window.AUTH=window.AUTH||{};window.AUTH["94-1966"]={"d":"94-1966","id":"1995/94-1966","n":"Loving v. United States","src":["osg"],"briefs":[{"k":"respondent","label":"Brief for the United States","date":"","url":"https://www.justice.gov/osg/media/221141/dl?inline","side":"res","src":"osg","fmt":"Text","detail":"https://www.justice.gov/osg/brief/dwight-j-loving-petitioner-v-united-states-america","toa":false,"n":110}],"opinion":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep517/usrep517748/usrep517748.pdf","tr":false,"items":[{"t":"case","label":"Biddle v. Perovich","cite":"274 U.S. 480","y":1927,"id":"scdb/1926-088","sc":1,"sum":"Under his power “ to grant reprieves and pardons for offenses against the United States ” (Const. Art II, § 2), the President may commute a sentence of death to life imprisonment, without the convict’s consent. Burdick v. United States, 236 U. S. 79, limited.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Bowsher v. Synar","cite":"478 U.S. 714","y":1966,"id":"1985/85-1377","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court found that the duties which the Congress delegated to the Comptroller General did violate the doctrine of separation of powers and were unconstitutional. A two step process led Chief Justice Burger to arrive at this conclusion.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Caldwell v. Parker","cite":"252 U.S. 376","y":1920,"id":"scdb/1919-089","sc":1,"sum":"The jurisdiction to try and punish for the crime of murder, committed by a person in the federal military service upon a civilian while the nation is at war, but in a place within the jurisdiction of a State where hostilities are not present and where martial law has not been proclaimed, is not vested exclusively in a military court-martial by the Articles of War of 1916; and conviction and sentence of a soldier, in …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Carter v. McClaughry","cite":"183 U.S. 365","y":1902,"id":"scdb/1901-126","sc":1,"sum":"The rule reiterated, that civil tribunals will not revise the proceedings of courts martial, except for the purpose of ascertaining whether they had jurisdiction of the person and of the subject matter, and whether though having such jurisdiction, they have exceeded their powers in’the sentences pronounced.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Coker v. Georgia","cite":"433 U.S. 584","y":1977,"id":"1976/75-5444","sc":1,"sum":"The Court ruled that use of the capital punishment as a penalty for rape was a form of cruel and unusual punishment forbidden by the Eighth Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Dames & Moore v. Regan","cite":"453 U.S. 654","y":1981,"id":"1980/80-2078","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the International Emergency Economic Powers Act constituted a specific congressional authorization for the President to order the transfer of Iranian assets. The Court further held that although the IEEPA itself did not authorize the presidential suspension of legal claims, previous acts of Congress had \"implicitly approved\" of executive control of claim settlement.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Dobbert v. Florida","cite":"432 U.S. 282","y":1977,"id":"1976/76-5306","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that, despite the fact that the Florida laws governing the death penalty changed during the time period the crimes in question were committed, the changes were procedural and better for the sentencing system.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Dreyer v. Illinois","cite":"187 U.S. 71","y":1902,"id":"scdb/1902-031","sc":1,"sum":"Dveyer was convicted in a state court of Illinois for having failed to turn over, as required by statute, to bis successor in office, certain revenues, bonds, funds, warrants and personal property, that came into his hands as Treasurer of a Board of Public Park Commissioners. The judgment of conviction was affirmed by the Supreme Court of Illinois, and the accused was sentenced to the penitentiary.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Field v. Clark","cite":"143 U.S. 649","y":1892,"id":"scdb/1891-139","sc":1,"op":3,"sum":"The signing by the Speaker of the House of Representatives and by the President of the Senate, in open session, of an enrolled bill, is an official attestation by the two Houses of such bill as one that has passed Congress; and when the bill thus attested receives the approval of the President, and is deposited in the Department of State according to law, its authentication as a bill that has passed Congress is …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Furman v. Georgia","cite":"408 U.S. 238","y":1972,"id":"1971/69-5030","sc":1,"op":3,"sum":"The Court found that the death penalty constituted cruel and unusual punishment in the case of an accidental burglary death.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Godfrey v. Georgia","cite":"446 U.S. 420","y":1980,"id":"1979/78-6899","sc":1,"sum":"The Supreme Court held that the Georgia law was unconstitutionally vague and failed to properly distinguish between those cases that would be eligible for the death penalty and those that wouldn’t. Even accepting the law, the Court found no evidence that Godfrey’s crimes contained the elements described in the law.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Goldman v. Weinberger","cite":"475 U.S. 503","y":1986,"id":"1985/84-1097","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Air Force regulation did not violate the Constitution.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Gregg v. Georgia","cite":"428 U.S. 153","y":1976,"id":"1975/74-6257","sc":1,"sum":"The Court ruled that the death penalty was not \"cruel and unusual\" punishment in violation of the Eighth and Fourteenth Amendments.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Haig v. Agee","cite":"453 U.S. 280","y":1981,"id":"1980/80-83","sc":1,"sum":"In a 7-to-2 decision, the Court held that Passport Act of 1926 and other congressional statutes implicitly granted the Secretary of State the power to revoke passports.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Hanna v. Plumer","cite":"380 U.S. 460","y":1965,"id":"1964/171","sc":1,"sum":"The Court ruled for Hanna. To raise the Erie doctrine, the Court reasoned, the effect of a procedural rule on the outcome of a case must abridge, enlarge, or modify the substantive law. There was no change to a substantive right here because the plaintiff simply could refile the case or serve the defendant personally.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Johnson v. Sayre","cite":"158 U.S. 109","y":1895,"id":"scdb/1894-191","sc":1,"sum":"In the Fifth Article of Amendments to the Constitútion of the United States, providing that “ no person shall be held to answer for a capital, or otherwise infamous crime, unless on presentment or indictment of a grand jury, except in cases arising in the land or naval forces, or in the militia, when in actual service in time of war or public danger,” the words “ whenin actual service in time of war or publicdanger” …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Jordan v. Watkins","cite":"681 F.2d 1067","y":1982,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/681/1067/","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Jurek v. Texas","cite":"428 U.S. 262","y":1976,"id":"1975/75-5394","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the death penalty was not per se a \"cruel and unusual\" punishment. Furthermore, the capital sentencing procedure in Texas was not unconstitutional on the theory that it would result in arbitrary and freakish impositions of the death penalty.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Kurtz v. Moffitt","cite":"115 U.S. 487","y":1885,"id":"scdb/1885-034","sc":1,"sum":"A writ of habeas corpus is not removable from a State court into a Circuit Court of the United States under the act of March 3, 1875, ch. 137, § 2.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Lewis v. Jeffers","cite":"497 U.S. 764","y":1990,"id":"1989/89-189","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that federal habeas corpus relief is not intended to find error in state laws but rather to determine whether a state court’s finding was so arbitrary and capricious that it violates the Eighth Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Lichter v. United States","cite":"334 U.S. 742","y":1948,"id":"scdb/1947-108","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"1. The Renegotiation Act is constitutional on its face as authority for the recovery by the United States of \"excessive profits\" (less tax credits) realized by private parties in the circumstances of these cases on subcontracts for war goods in time of war with contractors who were also private parties--even in the absence of contractual provisions for the renegotiation of such profits and even as applied to …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Lowenfield v. Phelps","cite":"484 U.S. 231","y":1988,"id":"1987/86-6867","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"At petitioner's state-court trial on charges of killing five people, the jury returned guilty verdicts on three counts of first-degree murder, an essential statutory element of which, under the circumstances, was a finding of intent \"to kill or inflict great bodily harm upon more than one person.\" At the penalty phase, in response to notes from the jury indicating difficulty in reaching a decision, the court twice …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Marsh v. Chambers","cite":"463 U.S. 783","y":1983,"id":"1982/82-23","sc":1,"sum":"As a consequence, the chaplaincy practice had become \"part of the fabric of our society.\" In such circumstances, an invocation for Divine guidance is not an establishment of religion. \"It is,\" wrote Burger, \"simply a tolerable acknowledgment of beliefs widely held among the people of this country.\".","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Maynard v. Cartwright","cite":"486 U.S. 356","y":1988,"id":"1987/87-519","sc":1,"sum":"As applied in this case, the statutory aggravating circumstance was unconstitutionally vague.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"McCleskey v. Kemp","cite":"481 U.S. 279","y":1987,"id":"1986/84-6811","sc":1,"sum":"The Court decided it was not a violation of the Eighth Amendment when purposeful discrimination could not be proven.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"McGautha v. California","cite":"402 U.S. 183","y":1971,"id":"1970/203","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner in No. 203 was convicted of first-degree murder in California, and was sentenced to death. The penalty was left to the jury's absolute discretion, and punishment was determined in a separate proceeding following the trial on the issue of guilt. Petitioner in No. 204 was convicted of first-degree murder, and was sentenced to death in Ohio, where the jury, which also had absolute penalty discretion, …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Middendorf v. Henry","cite":"425 U.S. 25","y":1976,"id":"1974/74-175","sc":1,"sum":"The Uniform Code of Military Justice (UCMJ) provides four methods of disposing of cases involving servicemen's offenses: general, special, and summary courts-martial, and disciplinary punishment pursuant to Art. 15 of the UCMJ. General courtsmartial and special courts-martial, which may impose substantial penalties, resemble judicial proceedings, nearly always presided over by lawyer judges, with lawyer counsel for …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Mistretta v. United States","cite":"488 U.S. 361","y":1989,"id":"1988/87-7028","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"Upholding delegation to the United States Sentencing Commission because of detailed congressional directives channeling agency discretion.","sumSrc":"opinion","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Moskal v. United States","cite":"498 U.S. 103","y":1990,"id":"1990/89-964","sc":1,"sum":"A person who receives genuine vehicle titles, knowing that they incorporate fraudulently tendered odometer readings, receives those titles knowing them to have been \"falsely made\" in violation of § 2314.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"NLRB v. Bell Aerospace Co.","cite":"416 U.S. 267","y":1974,"id":"1973/72-1598","sc":1,"sum":"On a petition by a labor union for a representation election, the National Labor Relations Board (NLRB) held that the buyers employed by respondent company constituted an appropriate collective-bargaining unit and directed an election. The NLRB stated that even though the buyers might be \"managerial employees\" they were nevprtheless covered by the National Labor Relations Act (NLRA) in the absence of any showing …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"O'Callahan v. Parker","cite":"395 U.S. 258","y":1969,"id":"1968/646","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"A crime, to be under military jurisdiction, must be service connected, and since petitioner's crimes were not, he could not be tried by court-martial but was entitled to a civilian trial with 'the benefits of an indictment by a grand jury and trial by jury.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Orloff v. Willoughby","cite":"345 U.S. 83","y":1953,"id":"scdb/1952-059","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Petitioner, a physician educated at government expense and beyond the usual draft age, was inducted into the Army under the Doctors' Draft Law, 50 U. S. C. App. § 454 (i), which authorizes special conscription of certain \"medical and allied specialist categories.\" Because of his refusal, on grounds of possible selfincrimination, to state in connection with his application for a commission whether he was or hact been …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Panama Refining Co. v. Ryan","cite":"293 U.S. 388","id":"1900-1940/293us388","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Writing for the 8-1 majority, Chief Justice Charles E. Hughes concluded the order was not constitutional. Supreme Court held that in enacting the order, the President subsumed legislative powers that Congress does not have the power to delegate. Congress cannot delegate legislative powers to other branches of government in order to sustain the democratic system of government.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Parisi v. Davidson","cite":"405 U.S. 34","y":1972,"id":"1971/70-91","sc":1,"sum":"The District Court should not have stayed its hand in this case.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Parker v. Levy","cite":"417 U.S. 733","y":1974,"id":"1973/73-206","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"Article 90 (2) of the Uniform Code of Military Justice (Code) provides for punishment of any person subject to the Code who \"willfully disobeys a lawful command of his superior commissioned officer\"; Art. 133 punishes a commissioned officer for \"conduct unbecoming an officer and a gentleman\"; and Art. 134 (the general article) punishes any person subject to the Code for, inter alia, \"all disorders and neglects to …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Poland v. Arizona","cite":"476 U.S. 147","y":1986,"id":"1985/85-5023","sc":1,"sum":"Reimposing the death penalty on petitioners did not violate the Double Jeopardy Clause.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Proffitt v. Florida","cite":"428 U.S. 242","y":1976,"id":"1975/75-5706","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the death penalty was not a \"cruel and unusual\" punishment per se, and that Florida's capital-sentencing procedure was not unconstitutionally arbitrary and/or capricious. Although empowering trial judges with sole sentencing authority, the statutory procedure tightly prescribed their relevant decision-making process.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Reid v. Covert","cite":"354 U.S. 1","y":1957,"id":"1955/701","sc":1,"op":5,"sum":"Citizen spouses of members of the U.S. military abroad are not subject to the U.S. Code of Military Justice but are entitled to Fifth and Sixth Amendment protections","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Reno v. Koray","cite":"115 S. Ct. 2021","y":1995,"id":"1994/94-790","sc":1,"sum":"The time respondent spent at the treatment center while \"released\" on bail was not \"official detention\" within the meaning of §3585(b).","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Romano v. Oklahoma","cite":"114 S. Ct. 2004","y":1994,"id":"1993/92-9093","sc":1,"sum":"The admission of evidence regarding petitioner's prior death sentence did not amount to constitutional error.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Rostker v. Goldberg","cite":"453 U.S. 57","y":1981,"id":"1980/80-251","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"In a 6-to-3 decision, the Court held that Congress's decision to exempt women from registration \"was not the 'accidental by-product of a traditional way of thinking about females'\" and did not violate the Due Process Clause. The Court found that men and women, because of combat restrictions on women, were not \"similarly situated\" for the purposes of draft registration.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Runkle v. United States","cite":"122 U.S. 543","y":1887,"id":"scdb/1886-306","sc":1,"sum":"Article 65 of tbe Articles of War in the act of April 10,1806, 2 Stat. 358,367, “ for the government of the armies of the United States,” enacted that “ neither shall any sentence.of a general court-martial, in time of peace, extending to the loss of life, or the dismission of a commissioned officer, or which shall, either in time of peace or war, respect a general officer, be carried into execution until after the …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Schick v. Reed","cite":"419 U.S. 256","y":1974,"id":"1974/73-5677","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Tracing the development and Court's interpretation of the President's powers to commute sentences, Chief Justice Burger argued that since the pardoning power is an enumerated one, any limit on it must be found in the Constitution. Thus, its use does not depend on statutes such as those found in the military code.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Schlesinger v. Councilman","cite":"420 U.S. 738","y":1975,"id":"1974/73-662","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"After court-martial charges were preferred against respondent Army captain for the sale, transfer, and possession of marihuana, he brought suit in Federal District Court to enjoin petitioner military authorities from proceeding with the court-martial. The District Court granted a permanent injunction, and the Court of Appeals affirmed, on the ground that the offenses charged were not \"service connected\" and hence …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Sedima, S.P.R.L. v. Imrex Co.","cite":"473 U.S. 479","id":"1984/84-648","sc":1,"sum":"The Racketeer Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (RICO), 18 U. S. C. §§ 1961-1968, which is directed at \"racketeering activity\"defined in § 1961(1) to encompass, inter alia,acts \"indictable\" under specific federal criminal provisions, including mail and wire fraud-provides in § 1964(c) for a private civil action to recover treble damages by any person injured in his business or property \"by reason of a …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Sibbach v. Wilson & Co.","cite":"312 U.S. 1","y":1941,"id":"scdb/1940-046","sc":1,"sum":"1. Congress has power to regulate the practice and procedure of federal courts, and may exercise it by delegating to the Supreme or other federal courts authority to make rules not inconsistent with the statutes or Constitution of the United States.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Skinner v. Mid-America Pipeline Co.","cite":"490 U.S. 212","id":"1988/87-2098","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Section 7005 of COBRA is not an unconstitutional delegation of the taxing power by Congress to the Executive Branch.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Tison v. Arizona","cite":"481 U.S. 137","y":1987,"id":"1986/84-6075","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that anticipating lethal force is not enough to satisfy the “intent to kill” requirement, but the Tisons major participation in the felony along with a reckless indifference to human life may be. Justice William J. Brennan wrote a dissent, expressing that this case illustrates how arbitrary capital sentencing is.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Touby v. United States","cite":"500 U.S. 160","y":1991,"id":"1990/90-6282","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"The Court held that the Controlled Substances Act allows the Attorney General to delegate authority to the Drug Enforcement Agency.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Tuilaepa v. California","cite":"114 S. Ct. 2630","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.%20Ct./114/2630/","sc":1,"sum":"Holding that the Constitu- \\par tion does not require that military judges have a fixed \\par term of office.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Flores","cite":"63 F.3d 1342","y":1995,"court":"5th Cir. \\par","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/63/1342/","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Grimaud","cite":"220 U.S. 506","y":1911,"id":"scdb/1910-122","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Under the acts establishing forest reservations, their use for grazing or other lawful purposes is subject to rules and regulations established by the Secretary of Agriculture, and it being impracticable for Congress to provide general regulations, that body acted within its constitutional power in conferring power on the Secretary to establish such rules; the power so conferred being administrative and not …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Mazurie","cite":"419 U.S. 544","y":1975,"id":"1974/73-1018","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Respondents, who operated a bar on non-Indian land on the outskirts of an unincorporated village within the Wind River Reservation and who had been denied a tribal liquor license by the Wind River Tribes pursuant to their option under 18 U. S. C. § 1161 to regulate the introduction of liquor into Indian country, were convicted at a nonjury trial of introducing spirituous beverages into Indian country in violation of …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"United States ex rel. Toth v. Quarles","cite":"350 U.S. 11","id":"1940-1955/350us11","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"He could not constitutionally be subjected to trial by court-martial.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Walton v. Arizona.","cite":"497 U.S. 639","y":1990,"id":"1989/88-7351","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Arizona method of death penalty sentencing is not arbitrary and does not place an undue burden on the defendant.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Walz v. Tax Comm'n","cite":"397 U.S. 664","y":1970,"id":"1969/135","sc":1,"sum":"In a 7-to-1 decision, the Court held that the exemptions did not violate the Establishment Clause. The Court held that the purpose of the exemptions was to neither advance nor inhibit religion; no one particular church or religious group had been singled out to receive tax exempt status.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Weiss v. United States","cite":"114 S. Ct. 752","y":1994,"id":"1993/92-1482","sc":1,"sum":"Mter courts-martial sentenced petitioners Weiss and Hernandez, United States Marines, on their pleas of guilty to offenses under the Uniform Code of Military Justice (UCMJ), their convictions were affirmed by the Navy-Marine Corps Court of Military Review in separate appeals. In affirming Weiss' conviction, the Court of Military Appeals rejected his contentions, first, that military trial and appellate judges have …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Yakus v. United States","cite":"321 U.S. 414","y":1944,"id":"1940-1955/321us414","sc":1,"sum":"The Supreme Court held that Congress sufficiently regulated the powers of the Administrator of the Act in such a way as to be a constitutional delegation of legislative power to obtain a particular objective. The Constitution grants Congress the necessary flexibility to perform its legislative function in a variety of ways.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Zant v. Stephens","cite":"462 U.S. 862","y":1983,"id":"1982/81-89","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Supreme Court held that where there are other valid aggravating circumstances to substantiate the death sentence, habeas relief is not available. The jury did not rely solely on the invalid factor in making its decision. Justice Byron R. White wrote a concurrence, stating that the sentence could stand on a line of cases where convictions are upheld if any of the counts are sustainable.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Stanford v. Kentucky","cite":"492 U.S. 361","id":"1988/87-5765","sc":1,"sum":"In a 5-to-4 decision the Court held that in weighing whether the imposition of capital punishments on offenders below the age of eighteen is cruel and unusual, it is necessary to look at the given society's evolving decency standards. With respect to American society, there is no national consensus regarding the imposition of capital punishments on 17- or 16-year-old individuals.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Arave v. Creech","cite":"113 S. Ct. 1534","y":1993,"id":"1992/91-1160","sc":1,"sum":"After respondent Creech pleaded guilty to first-degree murder for the brutal slaying of a fellow Idaho prison inmate, the state trial judge sentenced him to death based, in part, on the statutory aggravating circumstance that \"[b]y the murder, or circumstances surrounding its commission, the defendant exhibited utter disregard for human life.\" In affirming, the Idaho Supreme Court, among other things, rejected …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Ballew v. Georgia","cite":"435 U.S. 223","y":1978,"id":"1977/76-761","sc":1,"sum":"The Court found that a trial by jury of less than six members violated the accused's right to a trial by jury as protected by the Sixth and Fourteenth Amendments. Justice Blackmun reasoned that small juries foster poor group deliberation.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Branford v. United States","cite":"356 F.2d 876","y":1966,"court":"7th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/356/876/","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Burns v. Wilson","cite":"346 U.S. 137","y":1953,"id":"scdb/1952-107","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Judgment affirmed. Pp. 138-146. 91 U. S. App. D. C. 208, 202 F. 2d 335, affirmed. The District Court dismissed petitioners' applications for writs of habeas corpus. 104 F. Supp. 310, 312. The Court of Appeals affirmed. 91 U. S. App. D. C. 208, 202 F. 2d 335. This Court granted certiorari. 344 U. S. 903. At the time of the argument, February 5, 1953, Wilson, present Secretary of Defense, was substituted for Lovett, …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"California v. Brown","cite":"479 U.S. 538","y":1987,"id":"1986/85-1563","sc":1,"sum":"The instruction in question does not violate the Eighth and Fourteenth Amendments when given during the penalty phase of a capital murder trial.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Clemens v. Mississippi","cite":"494 U.S. 738","y":1990,"id":"1989/88-6873","sc":1,"sum":"At the sentencing hearing following petitioner Clemons' Mississippi capital murder conviction, the trial court instructed the jury, among other things, that, in deciding whether to impose the death penalty, it should consider the following statutory aggravating factors: (1) that the murder was committed during the course of a \"robbery for pecuniary gain,\" and (2) that it was an \"'especially heinous, atrocious or …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Harris v. Alabama","cite":"115 S. Ct. 1031","y":1995,"id":"1994/93-7659","sc":1,"sum":"The Eighth Amendment does not require the State to define the weight the sentencing judge must give to an advisory jury verdict.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Hildivin v. Florida","cite":"490 U.S. 638","y":1989,"id":"1988/88-6066","sc":1,"sum":"The Sixth Amendment does not require that the specific findings authorizing the imposition of the death sentence be made by a jury. Since the Court has held that the Amendment permits a judge to impose a death sentence when the jury recommends life imprisonment, Spaziano v. Florida,468 U. S. 447, it follows that the Amendment does not forbid the judge to make written findings authorizing the imposition of a death …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Jackson v. Virginia","cite":"443 U.S. 307","y":1979,"id":"1978/78-5283","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner was convicted of first-degree murder after a bench trial in a Virginia court, and his motion and petition in the state courts to set aside the conviction on the ground that there was insufficient evidence of premeditation, a necessary element of first-degree murder, were denied. He then brought a habeas corpus proceeding in Federal District Court, which, applying the \"no evidence\" criterion of Thompson v. …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"King v. Moseley","cite":"430 F.2d 732","y":1970,"court":"lOth Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/430/732/","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Mendrano v. Smith","cite":"797 F.2d 1538","y":1986,"court":"lOth Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/797/1538/","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Owens v. Markley","cite":"289 F.2d 751","y":1961,"court":"7th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/289/751/","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Ryder v. United States","cite":"115 S. Ct. 2031","y":1995,"id":"1994/94-431","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the judges' actions were not valid de facto. The Court reasoned that a defendant who made a timely challenge to the constitutional validity of the appointment of an officer who adjudicates his case was entitled to a decision on the merits of the question and whatever relief may be appropriate if a violation had occurred.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Solorio v. United States","cite":"483 U.S. 435","y":1987,"id":"1986/85-1581","sc":1,"op":12,"sum":"The jurisdiction of a court-martial depends solely on the accused's status as a member of the Armed Forces, and not on the \"service connection\" of the offense charged. Thus, O'Callahanis overruled. The plain meaning of Art. I, § 8, cl. 14, of the Constitution-which grants Congress plenary power \"[t]o make Rules for the Government and Regulation of the land and naval Forces\"-supports the military status test, as was …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Spaziano v. Florida","cite":"468 U.S. 447","y":1984,"id":"1983/83-5596","sc":1,"sum":"At petitioner's trial for first-degree murder, the Florida trial court informed him that it would instruct the jury on lesser included, noncapital offenses, if he would waive the statute of limitations, which had expired as to those offenses. Petitioner refused to waive the statute, and the jury was instructed solely on capital murder. After the jury returned a verdict of guilty of first-degree murder, a sentencing …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Stone v. INS","cite":"115 S. Ct. 1537","y":1995,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.%20Ct./115/1537/","sc":1,"by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Swaim v. United States","cite":"165 U.S. 553","y":1897,"id":"scdb/1896-115","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"It is within the power of the President, as commander-in-chief, to convene a general court-martial, even when the commander of the accused officer to be tried is not the accuser.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Chemical Foundation. Inc.","cite":"272 U.S. 1","id":"scdb/1926-001","sc":1,"sum":"1. A decree of the Circuit Court of Appeals, entered prior to the taking effect of the Jurisdictional Act of February 13, 1925, and affirming dismissal on the merits of a bill by the United States to set aside, as unauthorized and fraudulently procured, sales of patent and other rights and properties seized pursuant to the Trading, with the Enemy Act, was reviewable by this Court on appeal (Jud. Code §§ 128, 241). …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Whelchel v. McDonald","cite":"340 U.S. 122","y":1950,"id":"scdb/1950-011","sc":1,"sum":"The military tribunal that tried petitioner was not deprived of jurisdiction by the manner in which the insanity issue was dealt with, and habeas corpus was therefore not an available remedy.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Williams v. Florida.","cite":"399 U.S. 78","y":1970,"id":"1969/927","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that \"the 12-man [jury] requirement cannot be regarded as an indispensable component of the Sixth Amendment.\" The Court found that the purpose of the jury trial was \"to prevent oppression by the Government,\" and that the performance of this role was not dependent on the particular number of people on the jury.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer","cite":"343 U.S. 579","id":"1940-1955/343us579","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that the President did not have the right to seize control of most of the country's steel mills to negate the effects of a suspected strike during the Korean War.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"case","label":"Knauff v. Shaughnessy","cite":"338 U.S. 537","y":1950,"id":"scdb/1949-033","sc":1,"sum":"This action was authorized by the Act of June 21, 1941, 22 U. S. C. § 223, and the proclamations and regulations issued thereunder, notwithstanding the War Brides Act of December 28, 1945, 8 U. S. C. § 232 et seq.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"49 U.S.C. § 1473","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/49/1473","subs":["(c)(7)(1976)"],"by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3591","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3591","subs":["(1994)"],"by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 3592","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/3592","subs":["(c)(1994)"],"by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"29 U.S.C. § 141","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/29/141","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 801","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/801","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 818","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/818","stext":{"head":"§818. Art. 18. Jurisdiction of general courts-martial","paras":[[0,"(a) Subject to section 817 of this title (article 17), general courts-martial have jurisdiction to try persons subject to this chapter for any offense made punishable by this chapter and may, under such limitations as the President may prescribe, adjudge any punishment not forbidden by this chapter, including the penalty of death when specifically authorized by this chapter. General courts-martial also have jurisdiction to try any person who by the law of war is subject to trial by a military tribunal and may adjudge any punishment permitted by the law of war."],[0,"(b) A general court-martial of the kind specified in section 816(b)(3) of this title (article 16(b)(3)) shall not have jurisdiction to try any person for any offense for which the death penalty may be adjudged unless the case has been previously referred to trial as a noncapital case."],[0,"(c) Consistent with sections 819 and 820 of this title (articles 19 and 20), only general courts-martial have jurisdiction over the following offenses:"],[1,"(1) A violation of subsection (a) or (b) of section 920 of this title (article 120)."],[1,"(2) A violation of subsection (a) or (b) of section 920b of this title (article 120b)."],[1,"(3) An attempt to commit an offense specified in paragraph (1) or (2) that is punishable under section 880 of this title (article 80)."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/10/818?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§818. Art. 18. Jurisdiction of general courts-martial","tname":"Armed Forces","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 836","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/836","stext":{"head":"§836. Art. 36. President may prescribe rules","paras":[[0,"(a) Pretrial, trial, and post-trial procedures, including modes of proof, for cases arising under this chapter triable in courts-martial, military commissions and other military tribunals, and procedures for courts of inquiry, may be prescribed by the President by regulations which shall, so far as he considers practicable, apply the principles of law and the rules of evidence generally recognized in the trial of criminal cases in the United States district courts, but which may not, except as provided in chapter 47A of this title, be contrary to or inconsistent with this chapter."],[0,"(b) All rules and regulations made under this article shall be uniform insofar as practicable, except insofar as applicable to military commissions established under chapter 47A of this title."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/10/836?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":2,"head":"§836. Art. 36. President may prescribe rules","tname":"Armed Forces","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 851","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/851","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 855","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/855","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 856","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/856","stext":{"head":"§856. Art. 56. Sentencing","paras":[[0,"(a) Sentence Maximums.—The punishment which a court-martial may direct for an offense may not exceed such limits as the President may prescribe for that offense."],[0,"(b) Sentence Minimums for Certain Offenses.—(1) Except as provided in subsection (c) 1 of section 853a of this title (article 53a), punishment for any offense specified in paragraph (2) shall include dismissal or dishonorable discharge, as applicable."],[0,"(2) The offenses referred to in paragraph (1) are as follows:"],[1,"(A) Rape under subsection (a) of section 920 of this title (article 120)."],[1,"(B) Sexual assault under subsection (b) of such section (article)."],[1,"(C) Rape of a child under subsection (a) of section 920b of this title (article 120b)."],[1,"(D) Sexual assault of a child under subsection (b) of such section (article)."],[1,"(E) An attempt to commit an offense specified in subparagraph (A), (B), (C), or (D) that is punishable under section 880 of this title (article 80)."],[1,"(F) Conspiracy to commit an offense specified in subparagraph (A), (B), (C), or (D) that is punishable under section 881 of this title (article 81)."],[0,"(c) Imposition of Sentence.—"],[1,"(1) In general.—In sentencing an accused under section 853 of this title (article 53), a court-martial shall impose punishment that is sufficient, but not greater than necessary, to promote justice and to maintain good order and discipline in the armed forces, taking into consideration—"],[2,"(A) the nature and circumstances of the offense and the history and characteristics of the accused;"],[2,"(B) the impact of the offense on—"],[3,"(i) the financial, social, psychological, or medical well-being of any victim of the offense; and"],[3,"(ii) the mission, discipline, or efficiency of the command of the accused and any victim of the offense;"],[2,"(C) the need for the sentence—"],[3,"(i) to reflect the seriousness of the offense;"],[3,"(ii) to promote respect for the law;"],[3,"(iii) to provide just punishment for the offense;"],[3,"(iv) to promote adequate deterrence of misconduct;"],[3,"(v) to protect others from further crimes by the accused;"],[3,"(vi) to rehabilitate the accused; and"],[3,"(vii) to provide, in appropriate cases, the opportunity for retraining and return to duty to meet the needs of the service;"],[2,"(D) the sentences available under this chapter; and"],[2,"(E) the applicable sentencing parameters or sentencing criteria set forth in regulations prescribed by the President pursuant to section 539E(e) of the National Defense Authorization Act for Fiscal Year 2022."],[1,"(2) Application of sentencing parameters in general and special courts-martial.—"],[2,"(A) Requirement to sentence within parameters.—Except as provided in subparagraph (B), in a general or special court-martial in which the accused is convicted of an offense for which the President has established a sentencing parameter pursuant to section 539E(e) of the National Defense Authorization Act for Fiscal Year 2022, the military judge shall sentence the accused for that offense within the applicable parameter."],[2,"(B) Exception.—The military judge may impose a sentence outside a sentencing parameter upon finding specific facts that warrant such a sentence. If the military judge imposes a sentence outside a sentencing parameter under this subparagraph, the military judge shall include in the record a written statement of the factual basis for the sentence."],[1,"(3) Use of sentencing criteria in general and special courts-martial.—In a general or special court-martial in which the accused is convicted of an offense for which the President has established sentencing criteria pursuant to section 539E(e) of the National Defense Authorization Act for Fiscal Year 2022, the military judge shall consider the applicable sentencing criteria in determining the sentence for that offense."],[1,"(4) Offense-based sentencing in general and special courts-martial.—In announcing the sentence under section 853 of this title (article 53) in a general or special court-martial, the military judge shall, with respect to each offense of which the accused is found guilty, specify the term of confinement, if any, and the amount of the fine, if any. If the accused is sentenced to confinement for more than one offense, the military judge shall specify whether the terms of confinement are to run consecutively or concurrently."],[1,"(5) Inapplicability to death penalty.—Sentencing parameters and sentencing criteria shall not apply to a determination of whether an offense should be punished by death."],[1,"(6) Sentence of confinement for life without eligibility for parole.—"],[2,"(A) In general.—If an offense is subject to a sentence of confinement for life, a court-martial may impose a sentence of confinement for life without eligibility for parole."],[2,"(B) Term of confinement.—An accused who is sentenced to confinement for life without eligibility for parole shall be confined for the remainder of the accused's life unless—"],[3,"(i) the sentence is set aside or otherwise modified as a result of—"],[4,"(I) action taken by the convening authority or the Secretary concerned; or"],[4,"(II) any other action taken during post-trial procedure or review under any other provision of subchapter IX of this chapter;"],[3,"(ii) the sentence is set aside or otherwise modified as a result of action taken by a court of competent jurisdiction; or"],[3,"(iii) the accused receives a pardon or another form of Executive clemency."],[0,"(d) Appeal of Sentence by the United States.—(1) With the approval of the Judge Advocate General concerned, and consistent with standards and procedures set forth in regulations prescribed by the President, the Government may appeal a sentence to the Court of Criminal Appeals, on the grounds that—"],[1,"(A) the sentence violates the law;"],[1,"(B) in the case of a sentence for an offense for which the President has established a sentencing parameter pursuant to section 539E(e) of the National Defense Authorization Act for Fiscal Year 2022, the sentence is a result of an incorrect application of the parameter; or"],[1,"(C) the sentence is plainly unreasonable."],[0,"(2) An appeal under this subsection must be filed within 60 days after the date on which the judgment of a court-martial is entered into the record under section 860c of this title (article 60c)."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/10/856?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§856. Art. 56. Sentencing","tname":"Armed Forces","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 860","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/860","stext":{"head":"§860. Art. 60. Post-trial processing in general and special courts-martial","paras":[[0,"(a) Statement of Trial Results.—(1) The military judge of a general or special court-martial shall enter into the record of trial a document entitled \"Statement of Trial Results\", which shall set forth—"],[1,"(A) each plea and finding;"],[1,"(B) the sentence, if any; and"],[1,"(C) such other information as the President may prescribe by regulation."],[0,"(2) Copies of the Statement of Trial Results shall be provided promptly to the convening authority, the accused, and any victim of the offense."],[0,"(b) Post-trial Motions.—In accordance with regulations prescribed by the President, the military judge in a general or special court-martial shall address all post-trial motions and other post-trial matters that—"],[1,"(1) may affect a plea, a finding, the sentence, the Statement of Trial Results, the record of trial, or any post-trial action by the convening authority; and"],[1,"(2) are subject to resolution by the military judge before entry of judgment."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/10/860?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§860. Art. 60. Post-trial processing in general and special courts-martial","tname":"Armed Forces","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 866","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/866","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 871","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/871","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 880","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/880","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 906a","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/906a","stext":{"head":"§906a. Art. 106a. Wearing unauthorized insignia, decoration, badge, ribbon, device, or lapel button","paras":[[0,"Any person subject to this chapter—"],[1,"(1) who is not authorized to wear an insignia, decoration, badge, ribbon, device, or lapel button; and"],[1,"(2) who wrongfully wears such insignia, decoration, badge, ribbon, device, or lapel button upon the person's uniform or civilian clothing;"],[0,"shall be punished as a court-martial may direct."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/10/906a?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§906a. Art. 106a. Wearing unauthorized insignia, decoration, badge, ribbon, device, or lapel button","tname":"Armed Forces","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 918","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/918","stext":{"head":"§918. Art. 118. Murder","paras":[[0,"Any person subject to this chapter who, without justification or excuse, unlawfully kills a human being, when such person—"],[1,"(1) has a premeditated design to kill;"],[1,"(2) intends to kill or inflict great bodily harm;"],[1,"(3) is engaged in an act which is inherently dangerous to another and evinces a wanton disregard of human life; or"],[1,"(4) is engaged in the perpetration or attempted perpetration of burglary, rape, rape of a child, sexual assault, sexual assault of a child, aggravated sexual contact, sexual abuse of a child, robbery, or aggravated arson;"],[0,"is guilty of murder, and shall suffer such punishment as a court-martial may direct, except that if found guilty under clause (1) or (4), such person shall suffer death or imprisonment for life as a court-martial may direct, unless such person is otherwise sentenced in accordance with a plea agreement entered into between the parties under section 853a of this title (article 53a)."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/10/918?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":3,"head":"§918. Art. 118. Murder","tname":"Armed Forces","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 922","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/922","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 351","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/351","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 1111","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1111","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 1114","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1114","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"18 U.S.C. § 1751","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1751","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"21 U.S.C. § 848","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/21/848","subs":["(e)"],"by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1259","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1259","subs":["(1)"],"by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 816","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/816","subs":["(1)(A)"],"by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 852","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/852","subs":["(b)(l)"],"stext":{"head":"§852. Art. 52. Votes required for conviction, sentencing, and other matters","paras":[[0,"(a) In General.—No person may be convicted of an offense in a general or special court-martial, other than—"],[1,"(1) after a plea of guilty under section 845(b) of this title (article 45(b));"],[1,"(2) by a military judge in a court-martial with a military judge alone, under section 816 of this title (article 16); or"],[1,"(3) in a court-martial with members under section 816 of this title (article 16), by the concurrence of at least three-fourths of the members present when the vote is taken."],[0,"(b) Level of Concurrence Required.—"],[1,"(1) In general.—Except as provided in subsection (a) and in paragraph (2), all matters to be decided by members of a general or special court-martial shall be determined by a majority vote, but a reconsideration of a finding of guilty or reconsideration of a sentence, with a view toward decreasing the sentence, may be made by any lesser vote which indicates that the reconsideration is not opposed by the number of votes required for that finding or sentence."],[1,"(2) Sentencing.—A sentence of death requires (A) a unanimous finding of guilty of an offense in this chapter expressly made punishable by death and (B) a unanimous determination by the members that the sentence for that offense shall include death. All other sentences imposed by members shall be determined by the concurrence of at least three-fourths of the members present when the vote is taken."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/10/852?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":1,"head":"§852. Art. 52. Votes required for conviction, sentencing, and other matters","tname":"Armed Forces","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 837","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/837","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"usc","label":"10 U.S.C. § 898","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/10/898","by":{"0":""}},{"t":"const","label":"Article I","by":{"0":""}}]};